    {"about": "the words of a rate refused as a fraction", "decided": "One line naming --tax-rate, what the given rate would mean as a percent, and the percent it stands for, such as `--tax-rate is a percent, so \"0.19\" would be 0.19 %; write 19 for 19 %`, and `write 7.5 for 7.5 %` for 0.075. A rate written with a percent sign, such as 0.19%, is refused alike, since the decision draws the line by value alone.", "at": "invoicer/__main__.py:61"},
    {"about": "how a refusal names the tab", "decided": "Each refusal about the sheet, its header, or a cell begins `<file>: first sheet \"<tab name>\"`, the name from the sheet's table:name as Calc shows it on its tab, so the word first tells a user whose lines sit on a later tab that only the first is read. The refusal of a sheet with no lines names it the same way.", "at": "invoicer/sheet.py:53"},
    {"about": "what counts as a damaged file", "decided": "A zip whose mimetype is the .ods spreadsheet type but whose content.xml is missing, fails its checksum, won't decompress, isn't XML, or holds no sheet. Each gives `<file>: can't be read as a Calc spreadsheet; save it again from Calc as .ods`. A file that isn't a zip, or a zip without the .ods mimetype, keeps its refusal as not saved as .ods, since there the fix is choosing the .ods format in Save As.", "at": "invoicer/sheet.py:102"},
    {"about": "the property's rates", "decided": "The property test draws its rate from 0 or 1 to 100 to the tenth, since rates above 0 and below 1 are now refused before any invoice.", "at": "tests/test_main.py:257"},
